//? Title: Generalized Linear Models: Working Responses and Iris Probabilities
//? Subtitle: Hand calculations, IRLS diagnostics, separation and held-out logistic prediction with datamunge
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A binary species label needs a probability in the interval zero to one; an event count
needs a nonnegative mean. Ordinary regression does not enforce those different response
ranges or their different mean–variance relationships. Generalized linear models connect
a conditional mean to linear predictors through a link and fit it by iteratively
reweighted least squares. We calculate two tiny fits by hand, inspect every working
response and weight, check how repeated observations change uncertainty, and show why
numerical convergence cannot certify a finite logistic maximum under separation.
The iris analysis compares logistic models on the same training flowers and evaluates
probabilities on a separate test split. A final deliberately artificial count response
shows why rounding a measurement does not establish a Poisson sampling model. The
petal model correctly classifies 29 of 30 held-out flowers, with Brier loss 0.0254 and
log loss 0.0852; the sepal model classifies 22 correctly. These are results of one small,
prespecified split.
)

\toc

> The method

>> A response family, a mean and a link

For independent responses, a GLM specifies \(\mu_i=E(Y_i\mid x_i)\), a link
\(g(\mu_i)=\eta_i=x_i^\top\beta\), and a variance function
\(\operatorname{Var}(Y_i\mid x_i)=\phi V(\mu_i)/a_i\), where \(a_i>0\)
is a prior weight. An intercept is included unless the formula removes it. The four
fixed family/link pairs in `dm::stats::GLM` are:

| Family | Link \(g(\mu)\) | Variance function \(V(\mu)\) | Response |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Gaussian | \(\mu\) | \(1\) | finite continuous values |
| Binomial | \(\log[\mu/(1-\mu)]\) | \(\mu(1-\mu)\) | exactly 0 or 1 |
| Poisson | \(\log\mu\) | \(\mu\) | nonnegative counts |
| Gamma | \(1/\mu\) | \(\mu^2\) | strictly positive values |

These are R's default links; Gamma's inverse link is canonical up to sign and scale.
The implementation fixes \(\phi=1\) for binomial and Poisson, and estimates it for
Gaussian and Gamma. Categorical species labels must be encoded explicitly for a binomial
fit. Prior weights are not automatically survey weights or exposure offsets, and the
current API does not fit arbitrary links, offsets or grouped binomial proportions.

>> Deriving the update

Write the log density in exponential-family form

$$
 \ell(\beta,\phi)=\sum_i\frac{a_i\{y_i\theta_i-b(\theta_i)\}}{\phi}
                    +c_i(y_i,\phi,a_i),\qquad
 \mu_i=b'(\theta_i),\quad V(\mu_i)=b''(\theta_i).
$$
\alttext(The log likelihood separates a response times canonical parameter minus the cumulant, scaled by the prior weight and dispersion, from a remaining density term. The first two cumulant derivatives give the mean and variance function.)

The chain rule gives a score proportional to
\(X^\top\operatorname{diag}\{a_i/[V_i g'_i]\}(y-\mu)\).
The expected information is \(X^\top W X/\phi\), where
\(w_i=a_i/[V_i(g'_i)^2]\). A Fisher-scoring step can therefore be written
as a weighted least-squares problem:

$$
 z_i=\eta_i+(y_i-\mu_i)g'(\mu_i),\qquad
 w_i=\frac{a_i}{V(\mu_i)\,g'(\mu_i)^2},\qquad
 \beta_{\rm new}=\arg\min_b\sum_i w_i(z_i-x_i^\top b)^2.
$$
\alttext(The working response is the old linear predictor plus the response error times the link derivative. The next coefficients minimize weighted squared differences from those working responses.)

For logistic regression, \(w_i=a_i\mu_i(1-\mu_i)\); for Poisson,
\(w_i=a_i\mu_i\). Gaussian identity-link weights are simply \(a_i\),
and \(z_i=y_i\), recovering weighted least squares immediately. The library solves
each weighted system by Householder QR. Later steps that leave the finite family domain
or increase deviance are halved; an invalid first step throws because no preceding
feasible coefficient vector is available. This is a guarded local optimizer, not a
guarantee that every valid dataset admits a finite maximum.

`working_data(k)` reconstructs the inputs to update \(k+1\), starting at zero.
`iteration_history()` records completed updates, including any step fractions. Starting
means follow R's family conventions: binomial \((a_i y_i+1/2)/(a_i+1)\), Poisson
\(y_i+0.1\), and Gaussian/Gamma \(y_i\). Formula-null training rows are omitted
jointly, and `kept_rows()` retains their original zero-based indices. Nonfinite retained
values and nonpositive or nonfinite prior weights are errors.

>> Deviance, uncertainty and numerical stopping

For Bernoulli data and counts respectively, unit deviance is

$$
 d_B(y,\mu)=2\left[y\log\frac{y}{\mu}+(1-y)\log\frac{1-y}{1-\mu}\right],
 \qquad d_P(y,\mu)=2\left[y\log\frac{y}{\mu}-(y-\mu)\right],
 \qquad D=\sum_i a_i d(y_i,\mu_i).
$$
\alttext(Bernoulli deviance compares fitted and saturated binary likelihoods. Poisson deviance also subtracts the response minus fitted mean. Total deviance is their prior-weighted sum, with zero times log zero interpreted as zero.)

The Gaussian unit deviance is \((y-\mu)^2\); Gamma uses
\(2[-\log(y/\mu)+(y-\mu)/\mu]\). Raw deviance residuals are
\(\operatorname{sign}(y_i-\mu_i)\sqrt{a_i d_i}\), so their squared sum is
\(D\), including for weighted fits. The intercept-only null mean is the prior-weighted
response mean. With no intercept, the null linear predictor is zero instead.

At the returned fit, `covariance()` uses
\(\widehat\phi(X^\top W X)^{-1}\); \(\widehat\phi=1\) for binomial/Poisson,
and Pearson chi-square divided by \(n-p\) otherwise. Coefficient intervals are
Wald intervals, using a normal reference for fixed dispersion and a residual-df Student
reference otherwise. For a new design row \(x_0\), mean-response confidence limits are
constructed on the link scale:

$$
 s_\eta^2=x_0^\top\widehat{\operatorname{Cov}}(\widehat\beta)x_0,
 \qquad [L,U]=g^{-1}\{\eta_0\pm q_{1-\alpha/2}s_\eta\},
 \qquad s_\mu=\left|(g^{-1})'(\eta_0)\right|s_\eta.
$$
\alttext(The link-scale variance is a quadratic form in the coefficient covariance. Transforming the two link-scale confidence endpoints gives mean-response limits; the delta method gives the response-scale standard error.)

For a decreasing inverse link the endpoints are reordered. A Gamma interval crossing
zero on the link scale has an infinite upper response limit; a point prediction with
nonpositive Gamma linear predictor is rejected. Logistic limits stay between zero and
one. These are pointwise intervals for a conditional mean, not intervals containing a
future response with the stated probability.

`converged()` means only that
\(|D_{k+1}-D_k|/(0.1+|D_{k+1}|)<\texttt{tol}\). The iteration limit returns a
finite last iterate with that flag false, when numerical systems remain solvable.
`boundary()` flags binomial probabilities within \(10^{-8}\) of zero or one. It is a
warning heuristic, not a formal separation test. Training probabilities are bounded at
machine epsilon to keep computations finite; that guard does not supply a finite MLE.

Canonical-link likelihoods have negative-semidefinite Hessians on their domains.
Full rank gives strict curvature at an interior finite solution, but existence still
requires suitable data overlap. Complete logistic separation lets coefficient norms grow
while the likelihood approaches its supremum. Deviance changes can become tiny along
that path, so numerical convergence and existence of a maximum are different claims.

The current QR implementation explicitly accumulates an \(n\times n\) orthogonal
matrix. For \(K\) iterations and \(p<n\) columns, fitting uses
\(O(Kn^2p+Kp^3)\) work and \(O(n^2+np+Kp)\) storage, including the coefficient
trace. This implementation is intended for modest dense problems.

> Simple examples

>> Eight binary responses in two groups

At \(x=0\) observe \((0,0,0,1)\); at \(x=1\) observe \((0,1,1,1)\).
The binomial score equations set each group's fitted probability to its sample proportion:

$$
 \widehat\mu_0=\tfrac14,\quad \widehat\mu_1=\tfrac34,\quad
 \widehat\beta_0=-\log3,\quad \widehat\beta_1=2\log3,\quad
 X^\top W X=\begin{pmatrix}1.5&0.75\\0.75&0.75\end{pmatrix},\quad
 \operatorname{Cov}(\widehat\beta)=\begin{pmatrix}4/3&-4/3\\-4/3&8/3\end{pmatrix}.
$$
\alttext(The two fitted probabilities are one quarter and three quarters, giving intercept minus log three and slope twice log three. Each fitted observation has weight three sixteenths, yielding the displayed information and inverse matrices.)

Initially, zero responses have \(\mu=1/4\), \(\eta=-\log3\) and
\(z=-\log3-4/3\); one responses have the opposite \(\eta,z\). Every initial
weight is \(3/16\). Averaging \(z\) within the groups makes the first intercept
\(-[\log3+4/3]/2\) and slope \(\log3+4/3\). The table and figure expose that
first update before giving the converged fit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glm_binary_groups.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,0,0,0,1,1,1,1});
  d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,0,1,0,1,1,1});
  const dm::stats::GLM m(d,"y ~ x",{.family=dm::stats::GLMFamily::Binomial});
  const auto w=m.working_data(), trace=m.iteration_history();
  auto working=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  working.points(w.numeric_column("row").values(),w.numeric_column("z").values(),"working z");
  working.lines(std::vector<double>{0,3,4,7},std::vector<double>{trace.double_at("beta:(Intercept)",0),trace.double_at("beta:(Intercept)",0),
      trace.double_at("beta:(Intercept)",0)+trace.double_at("beta:x",0),trace.double_at("beta:(Intercept)",0)+trace.double_at("beta:x",0)},"first eta");
  working.title("Working response and first update").x_label("row (zero-based)").y_label("link scale").y_limits(-3,4.7);
  auto path=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  path.lines(trace.numeric_column("iteration").values(),trace.numeric_column("beta:(Intercept)").values(),"intercept",dm::RGB{37,99,235});
  path.lines(trace.numeric_column("iteration").values(),trace.numeric_column("beta:x").values(),"slope",dm::RGB{220,38,38});
  path.title("Approaching the exact coefficients").x_label("completed update").y_label("coefficient").y_limits(-1.5,3.6);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(working).add(path).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glm_binary_groups.svg");
  std::print("Initial working values:\n\n{}\n\nAccepted updates:\n\n{}\n\nFinal coefficients:\n\n{}\n",
      w.to_md(),trace.select({"iteration","deviance","beta:(Intercept)","beta:x"}).to_md(),m.coefficient_table().to_md());
}
```
