\caption(The fitted slope, scaled deviance and standard error under exact pattern replication. The line connects the closed-form standard errors at the displayed sample sizes.)
\alttext(The fitted standard errors coincide with the closed-form values and shrink as one over the square root of the replication count.)

>> A stopping flag does not rule out separation

Now take \(x=(-2,-1,1,2)\), \(y=(0,0,1,1)\). For intercept zero and
positive slope \(b\), every observed class is on the correct side, and

$$
 \ell(b)=-2\log(1+e^{-b})-2\log(1+e^{-2b})\longrightarrow0
 \quad\text{as }b\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
\alttext(The separated logistic log likelihood is minus twice log one plus exp minus b, minus twice log one plus exp minus two b. It increases toward zero as the slope grows without bound.)

Every finite \(b\) leaves \(\ell(b)<0\), proving that no finite MLE attains the
supremum. The fitted path below remains numerically finite, but the rising slope and
collapsing deviance show the degeneracy. The boundary flag and any convergence flag must
be read together; neither the epsilon guard nor extra iterations repairs the statistical
problem. Penalisation or bias reduction would define a different estimator.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glm_separation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d; d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-2,-1,1,2});
  d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,1,1});
  const dm::stats::GLM m(d,"y ~ x",{.family=dm::stats::GLMFamily::Binomial,.max_iter=50});
  const auto h=m.iteration_history();auto slope=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),dev=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  slope.lines(h.numeric_column("iteration").values(),h.numeric_column("beta:x").values());
  slope.title("A coefficient keeps growing").x_label("completed update").y_label("slope");
  dev.lines(h.numeric_column("iteration").values(),h.numeric_column("deviance").values());
  dev.title("Deviance approaches its infimum").x_label("completed update").y_label("deviance");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(slope).add(dev).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glm_separation.svg");
  auto status=m.model_summary().select({"n","iterations","converged","boundary"});
  status.add_column("deviance",std::vector<std::string>{std::format("{:.3e}",m.deviance())});
  std::print("{}\n",status.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | iterations | converged | boundary | deviance |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 4 | 23 | yes | yes | 3.567e-10 |

\image(figures/glm_separation.svg)
// result_end

\caption(A separated four-point sample. Deviance convergence can occur while the slope has no finite population-likelihood maximizer; the status table reports what the numerical algorithm actually did.)
\alttext(The slope rises steadily while deviance falls toward zero. The table separately reports iteration count, numerical convergence and a near-boundary probability warning.)

> Iris: two overlapping species

>> Fit probabilities on the original measurements

`dm::datasets::iris()` contains 150 flowers, 50 from each species, with four measurements
in centimetres. We retain the 100 versicolor and virginica flowers, encode virginica as
one, and fit `y ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width`. The response is an observed binary label,
not a count made from a measurement. Setosa is excluded because its clean separation
would turn this into the preceding nonexistence example rather than a regular coefficient
analysis. These probabilities describe the balanced two-species sample; they do not
establish prevalence or calibrated probabilities in another population.

The following full-data fit is descriptive. Its curve holds petal width at 1.75 cm; each
flower's fitted probability in the diagnostic panel uses its own length and width.
The confidence band conditions on the model and the fixed query width. The full-data
fit's boundary flag records extreme probabilities for some flowers. Both species still
overlap near the boundary; that heuristic does not diagnose separation, and the finite
coefficients agree with an independent R fit. Width 1.75 cm combined with very short
petals lies away from much of the observed joint predictor support, so the curve's
ends also illustrate conditional extrapolation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glm_iris_fit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::iris().filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")!="setosa";})
      .mutate_with("y",[](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")=="virginica" ? 1.0 : 0.0;});
  const dm::stats::GLM m(d,"y ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width",{.family=dm::stats::GLMFamily::Binomial});
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame grid; const auto length=dm::seql(3.0,7.0,201);
  grid.add_column("Petal.Length",length.values());grid.add_column("Petal.Width",std::vector<double>(201,1.75));
  const auto p=m.predict(grid,dm::stats::GLMPredictionInterval::Confidence);
  auto curve=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  curve.lines(length.values(),p.lower,"95% limits",dm::RGB{148,163,184});
  curve.lines(length.values(),p.upper,"",dm::RGB{148,163,184});curve.lines(length.values(),p.fit,"mean");
  curve.title("Petal width held at 1.75 cm").x_label("petal length (cm)").y_label("P(virginica)").y_limits(-.03,1.3);
  auto residuals=m.plot_residuals_vs_fitted();residuals.title("Bernoulli deviance residuals").x_label("fitted P(virginica)");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(curve).add(residuals).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glm_iris_fit.svg");
  std::print("Species summary (cm):\n\n{}\n\nCoefficients:\n\n{}\n\nFit status:\n\n{}\n",
      (d | dm::group_by("Species") | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","mean_length_cm"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Width","mean_width_cm")) | dm::to_md(3)),m.coefficient_table().to_md(),
      m.model_summary().select({"n","df","deviance","aic","iterations","converged","boundary"}).to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Species summary (cm):

| Species | n | mean_length_cm | mean_width_cm |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| versicolor | 50 | 4.260 | 1.326 |
| virginica | 50 | 5.552 | 2.026 |


Coefficients:

| term | estimate | std_error | statistic | p_value | lower | upper |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| (Intercept) | -45.272 | 13.612 | -3.326 | 0.001 | -71.951 | -18.594 |
| Petal.Length | 5.755 | 2.306 | 2.496 | 0.013 | 1.235 | 10.274 |
| Petal.Width | 10.447 | 3.756 | 2.782 | 0.005 | 3.086 | 17.808 |


Fit status:

| n | df | deviance | aic | iterations | converged | boundary |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |
| 100 | 97 | 20.564 | 26.564 | 8 | yes | yes |

\image(figures/glm_iris_fit.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Descriptive full-data logistic fit on the two overlapping iris species. Left: conditional probability and pointwise 95% mean-response limits at width 1.75 cm. Right: deviance residuals at each flower's own predictors.)
\alttext(The conditional probability rises steeply with petal length. Mean-response confidence limits widen around the transition. Binary deviance residuals form two branches according to the observed label.)

For a binary observation, the residual is
\(\sqrt{-2\log\widehat p}\) when \(y=1\), and
\(-\sqrt{-2\log(1-\widehat p)}\) when \(y=0\). The two curved branches are
therefore algebraically required, not by themselves evidence of misspecification or
normally distributed errors. A small training deviance does not estimate test accuracy.

>> One prespecified train/test comparison

We split once using `train_test_split(...,0.3,42,"Species")`, giving 35 training and
15 test flowers per species. The three candidates are fixed in advance: intercept only,
two sepals, and two petals. No response or fitted model sees the test labels during
training. Their AIC values are comparable because they use the same binary responses,
rows and Bernoulli likelihood. We do not compare these to a Gaussian density AIC on a
zero/one vector: a probability mass and a density use different reference measures.

Test probabilities are assessed by accuracy at the fixed threshold 0.5 and two proper
probability losses:

$$
 \operatorname{Brier}=\frac1m\sum_i(\widehat p_i-y_i)^2,\qquad
 \operatorname{logloss}=-\frac1m\sum_i\{y_i\log\widehat p_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-\widehat p_i)\}.
$$
\alttext(Brier loss averages squared probability errors. Log loss averages negative Bernoulli log probabilities of the observed classes. Lower values are better for both.)

The petal model's 29 correct classifications exceed the sepal model's 22 on this split;
its Brier loss 0.0254 and log loss 0.0852 are also lower. The training AIC ranking agrees
here, but the test set was not used to choose formulas or thresholds.

The score helper omits joint formula-null rows and reports their retained count. Its
scores are unweighted, including if the model was trained with prior weights. These
30 test flowers give one small, reproducible assessment, not a precise estimate of
future performance or a tuning set for selecting thresholds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glm_iris_holdout.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::iris().filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")!="setosa";})
      .mutate_with("y",[](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")=="virginica" ? 1.0 : 0.0;});
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(d,.3,42,"Species");
  const std::vector<std::string> formulas={"y ~ 1","y ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width","y ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width"};
  const std::vector<std::string> names={"intercept","sepals","petals"};
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame fits,scores;auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  for(std::size_t j=0;j<formulas.size();++j) {
    const dm::stats::GLM m(train,formulas[j],{.family=dm::stats::GLMFamily::Binomial});
    auto fit=m.model_summary().select({"n","deviance","aic","converged","boundary"});
    fit.add_column("model",std::vector<std::string>{names[j]});fits=j ? fits.concat_rows(fit) : fit;
    auto score=m.binomial_score(test);score.add_column("model",std::vector<std::string>{names[j]});
    scores=j ? scores.concat_rows(score) : score;
    if(j) {
      auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
      for(const std::string species : {"versicolor","virginica"}) {
        const auto group=test.filter([&](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")==species;});
        plot.points(group.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),m.predict(group),species,
            species=="versicolor" ? dm::RGB{37,99,235} : dm::RGB{220,38,38});
      }
      plot.abline_h(.5).title(names[j]+": held-out probabilities").x_label("petal length (cm)").y_label("P(virginica)").y_limits(-.04,1.35);
      layout.add(plot);
    }
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glm_iris_holdout.svg");
  std::print("Training fits:\n\n{}\n\nHeld-out scores:\n\n{}\n",fits.select({"model","n","deviance","aic","converged","boundary"}).to_md(),
      scores.select({"model","n","accuracy","brier","log_loss"}).to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Training fits:

| model | n | deviance | aic | converged | boundary |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |
| intercept | 70 | 97.041 | 99.041 | yes | no |
| sepals | 70 | 77.284 | 83.284 | yes | no |
| petals | 70 | 16.001 | 22.001 | yes | no |


Held-out scores:

| model | n | accuracy | brier | log_loss |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| intercept | 30 | 0.500 | 0.250 | 0.693 |
| sepals | 30 | 0.733 | 0.186 | 0.555 |
| petals | 30 | 0.967 | 0.025 | 0.085 |

\image(figures/glm_iris_holdout.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Training Bernoulli AIC and separate test scores for the three prespecified models. Panels show each test flower's fitted probability against its petal length; colours are observed species and the horizontal line is the fixed classification threshold.)
\alttext(Two panels compare held-out probabilities from sepal and petal predictors. Observed species colours show mistakes around the one-half threshold, and the tables report accuracy, Brier loss and log loss.)

>> A deliberate Poisson mismatch

To retain the old example's count demonstration, define `count = nearbyint(Sepal.Length)`
on all 150 flowers. These numbers are rounded centimetres, not counts of independently
occurring events over a fixed exposure. A log-link fit can produce positive means, but
that range property does not establish the Poisson variance assumption.

For a correctly specified fixed-dispersion count model, Pearson chi-square divided by
residual degrees of freedom is often used as a rough dispersion diagnostic. A very small
ratio here reflects the restricted spread created by rounding lengths. Discrete bands
in a residual plot are expected for a response with few distinct values; they do not
by themselves establish adequacy. We show the ratio without estimating a free Poisson
dispersion or using rounded measurements as biological count evidence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glm_iris_rounded.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::iris().mutate_with("count",[](const auto& r){return std::nearbyint(r.get_double("Sepal.Length"));});
  const dm::stats::GLM m(d,"count ~ Sepal.Width + Petal.Length",{.family=dm::stats::GLMFamily::Poisson});
  double pearson=0;for(double r:m.pearson_residuals())pearson+=r*r;
  auto status=m.model_summary().select({"n","df","deviance","dispersion","converged"});
  status.add_column("Pearson_X2/df",std::vector<double>{pearson/m.degrees_of_freedom()});
  auto fit=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  fit.points(m.fitted_values(),d.numeric_column("count").values(),"rounded length");
  fit.abline(0,1).title("Rounded lengths are not event counts").x_label("fitted mean").y_label("rounded sepal length").x_limits(3.5,8.5).y_limits(3.5,9.5);
  auto residuals=m.plot_residuals_vs_fitted();residuals.title("Discrete deviance-residual bands");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(fit).add(residuals).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glm_iris_rounded.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n",status.to_md(),m.coefficient_table().select({"term","estimate","std_error"}).to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | df | deviance | dispersion | converged | Pearson_X2/df |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 150 | 147 | 4.792 | 1 | yes | 0.033 |


| term | estimate | std_error |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| (Intercept) | 1.203 | 0.308 |
| Sepal.Width | 0.085 | 0.086 |
| Petal.Length | 0.079 | 0.021 |

\image(figures/glm_iris_rounded.svg)
// result_end

\caption(A Poisson fit to rounded sepal lengths is an API illustration and a variance-assumption counterexample. Dispersion remains fixed at one by the chosen family; the separately computed Pearson ratio diagnoses the mismatch.)
\alttext(Rounded sepal lengths form horizontal stripes against their fitted means. Deviance residuals form discrete bands, and the Pearson chi-square per residual degree of freedom is far below one.)

> Conclusion

The tiny group and count fits expose exactly what IRLS solves, while replication checks
its information scaling. The separated sample demonstrates why a tiny deviance change
cannot certify a finite logistic MLE. On iris, comparing models within a common Bernoulli
likelihood and evaluating a separate test split gives interpretable probability scores.
Mean-response confidence limits, training fit, numerical convergence and held-out prediction
answer different questions. Neither a successful optimizer nor positive fitted means can
validate an assumed response distribution; the rounded-length example makes that limit visible.

> References

- Nelder, J. A. and Wedderburn, R. W. M. (1972). [Generalized Linear Models|https://doi.org/10.2307/2344614]. ~Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series A~, 135(3), 370–384. Exponential-family formulation and iterative fitting.
- Albert, A. and Anderson, J. A. (1984). [On the existence of maximum likelihood estimates in logistic regression models|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/71.1.1]. ~Biometrika~, 71(1), 1–10. Existence and separation.
- R Core Team. [Fitting Generalized Linear Models|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/glm.html], [Family Objects|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/family.html] and [Akaike's An Information Criterion|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/AIC.html]. Defaults, diagnostic conventions and restrictions on likelihood comparison.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. ~Annals of Eugenics~, 7(2), 179–188. Iris measurements; the built-in dataset is used directly.

